I wrote this this book mainly to to  think about how we ought to be in as an  unashamed made lifelong member of the  Labour Party that is that's where I'm  coming from how we ought to be able to  think about the future and start  preparing for the era that's coming and  part of that I thought was a critique of  where we are now and how we've managed  to evolve our society in a context that  is really about what I call market  fundamentalism in the book the unwritten  market fundamentalist rules and I'll  talk about them in a minute I wanted to  talk today about a more positive view of  where I think we should be going as  Democratic socialists and I'm  unashamedly a Democratic Socialist I'm  not a communist and I'm not a vanguard  isten I'm not too Leninist and I'm not  any of those things I don't believe that  you can develop a society that's  democratic and socialist without the  consent of people in it or out with a  democratic system and I think that there  are five main values which when I  thought about what socialism meant to me  as a lifelong member of the Labour Party  we would have to try to apply when we  were thinking about what kind of society  want to create in the 21st century  rather than resting on the laurels of  the society we created in the 19th or  the 20th and those values in no  particular order are equality democracy  Liberty cooperation and internationalism  and I think that everything that I tried  to say in the book needs to be  encompassed in making those values real  for everybody in our society so what I  thought it would be  interesting to do is just for me to talk  a little bit about some of the things I  think we need to change and then say a  few words about areas where that change  might manifest itself just to give you  an idea of what's in my mind about how  we shape the development of our of our  society so I think the first thing to  say is that you have to have I mentioned  it this morning I said be a citizen of  nowhere you have to have a view from  nowhere  in other words you have to get away from  what the essential view that we've had  since the triumph of Thatcherism  happened 40 years ago of the motivations  of individuals in society and that is  that somehow we are all in it to  maximize our own advantage and our own  wealth and if as individuals we do that  then society will miraculously  automatically be the best it can i  profoundly disagree with that kind of  analysis and I actually think that human  beings actually set themselves in a much  more social context than that they set  themselves in a much more collectivist  context than that because they actually  do have what Thomas Nagel the  rationalist philosopher called a view  from nowhere and that is they have the  morality and ethics such that they have  an objective view of other people's  circumstances and the position they're  in so they're not pursuing only we  aren't pursuing our own satisfaction and  utility constantly with no regard to  anyone else actually in varying degrees  we have a lot of regard for other people  and if we accept that as a principle  from which we organize society  it creates an ethical and compassionate  view which I think starts to animate  some of the values that I was talking  about earlier so unlike Teresa May I  don't believe that if you're not a  citizen of somewhere you're a citizen of  nowhere and somehow that's a bad thing  I also think by the way that having that  moral view will become increasingly  important as the world struggles with  issues like climate change and the  interact there interrelatedness of our  globe which is getting more and more  into relate to rather than less and less  so without that view I think it's very  difficult to really assert yourself as a  society to do anything useful for your  own society or anyone else and so that's  the first thing I think that there are  some things that need to change at a  high level about our politics to make  them different so I just want to use a  few examples firstly I would say the  empowerment of women and in the book I  call that completing the Enlightenment  because I kind of agree with Mary  Wollstonecraft and she thought when the  Enlightenment ideas were developed that  it included women that the French  Revolution when it talked about  fraternity a gala tea all of that that  women were included only to be horrified  to find out that Talleyrand and all the  French revolutionaries only wanted all  of that for men which is why she wrote a  vindication of the rights of women to  begin with and I think it's about time  the feminism and 51% of the population  got to have their fair and equal go at  helping to run the world so that's the  first thing I think that would actually  transform a lot of the way that things  are done and when I talk about women as  well I'm going to include those other  minorities not majorities which are all  avulsed tended to be excluded from power  so people who are discriminated against  people with disabilities all of the  range of discriminations that are  protected in law need to be brought into  our political system so that we can hear  their voices and accommodate to their  needs wants and and desires so that's  number one  I  think number two we need to get away  from the narrow utilitarian view of  politics which has come about as a  result of our marketized approach to  economics and society and that is  somehow that politics is a choice of  brands that you can choose one or the  other that you have a retail view of  politics --is who's got the best adverts  who can persuade you to bright by blue  trainers or red trainers and then you  can pick one or the other but you've all  always got to have trainers and nothing  really changes because the trainers are  provided by particular people and and  companies that you have no effect on I  don't think that's a reasonable way of  politics I've always said about politics  if you don't do politics you get it done  to you and so a lot of the people that  are excluded from power that are  excluded from positions of influence  don't feel that politics is for them and  there's a lot of there there are the  people that are the vested interests  that want to keep it that way and you  actually get real change and development  in society when those people who don't  do politics stop thinking it's a  spectator sport and start thinking you  know what I'm not cheering on my side  from the stands I ought to be on the  pitch and if you're on the pitch and you  can persuade people to get involved in  politics in that way you can have major  transformation of the sort that I don't  think many of us have likely to have  experienced in our lifetimes to date so  let's get rid of the technocratic  utilitarian retail view of politics and  that opens up a Vista of possibilities  for proper organization of debate and  how we actually change our politics for  the good I think also it's important  that we think about a complete change in  the way that we regard economics  as a Democratic Socialist I think the  way we run our economy the labor market  we have the kind of structures we have  in our society a really important part  of whether we can empower people that's  to help people who are from the working  class get on to ensure that everyone has  the right to reach their full potential  in life and also contribute to the  common good that we can develop in our  society so it's not about individual  striving only it is about how we can all  try to contribute to the common good and  so I think when we look at the way that  economics works we also see that very  narrow utilitarian neoclassical approach  if I could call it that Homo economicus  I think the phrase is and it is a homo a  man what I mean when I say that  obviously an economic man and the  utilitarian implications of how that  works have narrowed our politics and the  possibilities of change for a very very  long time they are profit maximizing  utility maximizing always fighting  everyone else for their bit of the pie  there's no real attempt to create a  system that is much more holistic for  more debates talks and interviews  subscribe today to the Institute's of  Arts and ideas at IAI TV  you
 

